<article class="env-proposition" id="06KM">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06KM">Proposition <span data-tag="06KM">9.4.7.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an accessible $\infty $-category. Then there are arbitrarily large (small) regular cardinals $\lambda $ for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\lambda $-accessible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is accessible, it is $\kappa $-accessible for some small regular cardinal $\kappa $. Combining Propositions <a data-tag="06KA" href="/tag/06KA">9.4.7.5</a> and <a data-tag="061N" href="/tag/061N">9.1.7.8</a>, we conclude that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\lambda $-accessible for every small regular cardinal $\lambda $ of exponential cofinality $\geq \kappa $ (for example, every cardinal of the form $(\mu ^{\kappa })^{+}$). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
